paragraphs.rhyme:
By night the three to [Zhu Jun](char-zhu-jun)'s camp drew nigh,
With troops behind, beneath the open sky.
[Jun](char-zhu-jun) warmly welcomed them, his troops to spread,
And against [Zhang Bao](char-zhang-bao) his joined forces led.
Meanwhile [Cao Cao](char-cao-cao) with [Huangfu Song](char-huangfu-song) was bound
'Gainst [Zhang Liang](char-zhang-liang)'s host on Quyang's bloody ground.
Here [Zhu Jun](char-zhu-jun) pressed [Zhang Bao](char-zhang-bao) with all his might;
Eight or nine myriads stood beyond the height.
[Liu Bei](char-liu-bei), by [Jun](char-zhu-jun) appointed for the van,
Drew up against the foe, his standard ran.
[Bao](char-zhang-bao) sent Gao Sheng, his deputy, to dare;
[Liu Bei](char-liu-bei) sent [Zhang Fei](char-zhang-fei) forth, the foe to bear.
[Fei](char-zhang-fei) spurred his horse, his pointed spear he plied;
Few rounds, and Gao Sheng falling rolled, and died.
[Liu Bei](char-liu-bei) waved on his men, with shout and call;
And straight through [Bao](char-zhang-bao)'s array they swept o'er all.
But [Bao](char-zhang-bao), his hair loose-streaming, sword in fist,
On horseback wrought a dread sorcerous mist.
A storm of wind and thunder broke around;
A column dark from heaven swept the ground;
Within that vapor phantom horsemen ride,
Innumerable warriors, side by side.
[Liu Bei](char-liu-bei) in haste recall'd his troops in flight;
The army broke in chaos, lost the fight.
Return'd to camp; [Jun](char-zhu-jun)'s voice in counsel rose:
"To-morrow shall his magic meet its close.
Slay pig and sheep and dog, their blood to take;
And place it on the hilltop, ambush make.
When rebels chase us, pour the blood like rain;
His sorcerous arts shall break, and prove in vain."

[Jun](char-zhu-jun) heeded; from the east and south he drew
His forces back, and to the northwest flew.
[Han Zhong](char-han-zhong), as foreseen, fled the city wide;
And out he led his troops, no place to hide.
[Jun](char-zhu-jun) with the brothers three pursu'd in chase;
[Han Zhong](char-han-zhong) was shot — the rest fled all apace.

Next morn, [He Jin](char-he-jin) was rais'd to high command:
The Records of the court, his to demand.
The rest were granted offices that day,
Each took an office, each receiv'd his pay.
Now Dow'ger [Dong](char-empress-dowager-dong), with secret call, had brought
[Zhang Rang](char-zhang-rang) to council; gravely there besought:
"[He Jin](char-he-jin)'s own sister — once I rais'd her high;
Her son rules now — the realm beneath his eye;
All ministers, of inner court and out,
Are her own confidants — her power is stout.
What course remains?" [Rang](char-zhang-rang) answer'd in reply:
"Your Majesty may sit where curtains lie
Let down, and listen to the state's affair;
Enfeoff [Prince Xie](char-prince-xie), and keep him in thy care;
Let [Dong Zhong](char-dong-zhong), kinsman, hold the armies' guide;
Trust us, and great designs may then be tried."
